Yes, that is a good feature, and it is very common in Snake games. The idea is:

- the game starts with a base speed
- each time the snake eats food, the interval becomes slightly smaller
- this makes the snake move faster

## The core idea

Right now you likely have something like this:

```js
gameInterval = setInterval(gameLoop, 150);
```

The number `150` is the delay in milliseconds. Smaller delay = faster movement.

So when the snake eats:

```js
score += 1;
```

you can also reduce the delay:

```js
clearInterval(gameInterval);
gameSpeed = Math.max(60, gameSpeed - 5);
gameInterval = setInterval(gameLoop, gameSpeed);
```

## Example setup

```js
let gameSpeed = 150;
```

Then:

```js
const increaseSpeed = () => {
    clearInterval(gameInterval);
    gameSpeed = Math.max(60, gameSpeed - 5);
    gameInterval = setInterval(gameLoop, gameSpeed);
};
```

Then inside the food check:

```js
if (head.x === food.x && head.y === food.y) {
    score += 1;
    increaseSpeed();
    food = generateFood();
}
```

## Why `Math.max(60, ...)`

This keeps the speed from becoming too fast. It prevents the delay from going below `60ms`.

## Important note

When the game resets, set the speed back to the original starting value:

```js
gameSpeed = 150;
```

This ensures every new game starts at the same speed.

## Best practice

Create a dedicated function like `increaseSpeed()` instead of writing the logic directly inside the food check. That keeps the code cleaner and easier to maintain.

This is a strong upgrade because it makes the game feel more dynamic and challenging without changing the rules.

