Yes — you can choose either one, or both.

## Option 1: only the spacebar
This is the simplest and matches your idea.

- Press Space to pause
- Press Space again to resume
- No button needed

Example:

```js
document.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
    if (event.code === "Space") {
        event.preventDefault();

        if (isGameOver) {
            return;
        }

        isPaused = !isPaused;

        if (isPaused) {
            info.textContent = "Paused";
        } else {
            info.textContent = "";
        }
    }
});
```

This works well and is enough for a small game.

## Option 2: button + spacebar
This is more user-friendly, especially for players who do not know the shortcut.

You can add:

```html
<button id="pauseBtn">Pause</button>
```

and then connect it to the same logic as the spacebar:

```js
const togglePause = () => {
    if (isGameOver) return;

    isPaused = !isPaused;

    if (isPaused) {
        info.textContent = "Paused";
        pauseBtn.textContent = "Resume";
    } else {
        info.textContent = "";
        pauseBtn.textContent = "Pause";
    }
};

pauseBtn.addEventListener("click", togglePause);

document.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
    if (event.code === "Space") {
        event.preventDefault();
        togglePause();
    }
});
```

## Recommendation
For your assignment, I would recommend:

- use the spacebar as the pause/resume control
- optional extra: add a visible button too if you want the UX to feel more polished

So the answer is: no, you do not need a button if you are using the keyboard only. But adding both is perfectly fine and more user-friendly.

