`event.preventDefault()` stops the browser’s default action for that event.

For example, when you press the spacebar on a page:

- the browser may scroll the page down
- the page may also trigger focus changes or other default behavior

Using:

```js
event.preventDefault();
```

prevents that default behavior so your game controls work as intended.

Example:

```js
document.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
    if (event.code === "Space") {
        event.preventDefault();
        togglePause();
    }
});
```

Without `preventDefault()`, pressing Space might scroll the page while also trying to pause the game. That would be annoying and make the game feel broken.

So in simple words:

> `preventDefault()` says: “Do not perform the browser’s normal action for this key press; I will handle it myself.”

This is common in game code, especially for keys like Space, arrow keys, and Enter.

