Yes, that approach will work. Small terminology note: `if`/`else` is a **conditional**, not a loop. It chooses which screen to show based on `screenName`.

```js
const homeScreen = document.getElementById("homeScreen");
const gameScreen = document.getElementById("gameScreen");

const showScreen = (screenName) => {
    if (screenName === "game") {
        homeScreen.classList.remove("active");
        gameScreen.classList.add("active");
    } else if (screenName === "home") {
        gameScreen.classList.remove("active");
        homeScreen.classList.add("active");
    }
};

playButton.addEventListener("click", () => showScreen("game"));
homeButton.addEventListener("click", () => showScreen("home"));
```

Your `classList.remove()` call needs quotes around the class name: `remove("active")`.

The class only changes visibility if your CSS defines it. For example:

