### Step 1: Keep food off the snake

Update `generateFood()` so it picks a position, then checks whether any snake segment already occupies it. If so, it tries again:

```js
function generateFood() {
  let foodX;
  let foodY;

  do {
    foodX = Math.floor(Math.random() * tileCount);
    foodY = Math.floor(Math.random() * tileCount);
  } while (snake.some(segment => segment.x === foodX && segment.y === foodY));

  return { x: foodX, y: foodY, visible: true };
}
```

`do...while` runs the random-position code at least once, then repeats while the condition is true. `snake.some(...)` checks whether at least one segment has the same `x` and `y` as the candidate. This works even if the function is declared before `snake`: what matters is that `snake` is initialized before `generateFood()` is called.

One edge case: if the snake fills every cell, this loop would keep trying forever. You can add full-board handling later; it’s unlikely to matter while building the basic game.

