The missing braces go after the inner loop’s body: close the inner loop, then the outer loop. Put `return true` after both loops so every footprint cell is checked before the direction is accepted.

```js
const validDirections = possibleDirections.filter((dir) => {
  for (let w = 0; w < width; w++) {
    for (let h = 0; h < height; h++) {
      // Check this footprint cell.
      if (/* blocked */) return false;
    }
  }

  return true;
});
```

The inner `for` checks each `h` value for one `w`; the outer `for` advances `w`. Returning `false` rejects the direction as soon as any cell is blocked. Returning `true` after both loops accepts it only after all cells pass. The `});` then closes the filter callback and the `filter` call.

Errors reported afterward are likely cascading parser errors from the unclosed loops, rather than separate problems.

